      "id": "architecture-of-attention",
      "url": "https://example.test/essays/architecture-of-attention/",
      "title": "The architecture of attention",
      "content_text": "What public rooms can teach digital products about pace, focus, and belonging.",
      "content_html": "<p>Every room makes an argument about how we should move through it. A doorway slows us down; a bench gives us permission to stay.</p><h2>Designing for arrival</h2><p>The most generous spaces acknowledge that attention has a body. They create thresholds, landmarks, and places to pause instead of demanding immediate action.</p><blockquote>Good interfaces can feel less like instructions and more like invitations.</blockquote><p>Building this way requires restraint, but it also creates trust. People know where they are and why they might continue.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-08-19T15:00:00Z",
      "image": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Architecture_photography.jpg",
      "authors": [{"name": "Nora Bell"}],
      "_microfeed": {
        "date_published_short": "Aug 19, 2026",
